Original conversation material (`current`): [the Tape write-boundary repair](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-preserve-conversation-material.md) preserves full user intent and assistant response text, including empty strings. Previously the display-summary limit discarded their tails before the next Run or Context View could read them. Structural summaries and payload controls are unchanged; old clipped rows are not reconstructed. `scripts/m5-history-material-regression.el` verifies actual second-Run input, SQLite reopen, whole-item budgets and inspected deletion. Buffer listing now quotes exact names, and retryable feedback explicitly returns to the original task after correcting a failed call.

The full configuration trials on the preceding source completed all six Runs but did not pass either entry's complete apply/undo acceptance; the candidate operating note also failed its gate. Their frozen results are in `.scratch/m5-config-acceptance/RESULTS-20260917.md`. Those trials exercised batch finalization at 12 rounds. The final 5291b745 B trials then exercised one normal extension per Run under the unchanged deadlines: chat did not establish the binding, while act established it live and after restart but also modified the forbidden source file. Act undo restored the original observed state. All four Runs completed without timeout, but neither entry passed the full task. Actual undo HTTP carried the preceding full answer in both paths. Results are in `.scratch/m5-config-acceptance/FINAL-INTERACTIVE-RESULTS.md`; this source predates the missing-response terminal repair. The bounded check of the unchanged 1024-byte note on the same 5291b745 source and interactive conditions passes every automatic chat apply/undo state check, but the complete answers still contain verifiable API/file-state errors. Under its pre-registered gate, no C/act follows and the note is not promoted into the product. This is evidence of useful task behavior in one scenario, not a stable success rate or a reference-reader decision. The [development handoff](reports/2026-09-17-m5-execution-handoff.md) records final code verification, source-separated real results and the remaining task/contract boundaries.

Its discovery and origin tests live in `scripts/m5-regression.el`, the second leader-owned test machine: the user ruled on 2026-09-16 that new tests are not added to `scripts/m3-regression.el` any more. The set builds its own fixture on the canonical smoke helpers and is scripted-only, so it skips under `SMOKE_REAL`; real-model evidence belongs to each slice's own end-to-end run.

Verification (slice 1): the M5 set's three discovery tests pass — the default request offers the tool, a model scripted to call it gets a known function's real documentation back with nothing asked, and a user who set `prompt-for-all-reads` is still asked — with a fourth test guarding that a request naming its own tools is not widened. Mutants M57 to M60 each turn their target red. The M3 set's 139 tests, the canonical smoke set with its baselines untouched, and the Kernel check all pass; byte-compile warnings are identical to the baseline; and against a real provider (LM Studio `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`) the M3 live turns and every M4 test still pass, so the default experience is unchanged.

Removes: nothing. No capability leaves the default set, no user configuration is rewritten, and an explicit tool list, a stricter approval policy or a narrowed Preset keeps its meaning — an upgrade never appends to a list the user set.

Closure (2026-09-19): the acceptance below is met, within stated limits. Under M7's division of labour — the model edits the buffer and makes the change live, the user reviews the diff and saves, an independent checker restarts — the configuration task with its undo passed from the chat window (`m7-attended-chat-04-deepseek`) and from the current buffer (`m7-attended-act-06-deepseek`), all eleven checks in both phases of both entries, with `deepseek-chat`, a person answering every approval and both save reviews, and the leader's review of both final answers ([closure report](reports/2026-09-19-m5-acceptance-closure.md)). **Guaranteed:** one complete pass per entry, each on its own recorded product commit (chat on `7e368f5b`, act on `9c0c3c3e`; the chat entry was not re-run on the final commit), with the recorded model configuration and review procedure. **Not guaranteed:** a success rate — the six act runs are run history across different products, harness versions and human save decisions, not a sample from which a rate can be estimated; other models — the local 27B model has two valid failures on the chat entry and is not a configuration this acceptance passed with; the model's recovery after a refused save, which no run exercised once the contradictory tool description was gone. Slices 3 and 4 (a reference-reading carrier and an operating guide) stay `proposed`: the accepted scope did not need them to pass, which is evidence about this task and this model, not a decision against them. The observation that a reply announcing the next step with no tool call ends a Run (twice in six act runs with this model, raw responses not recoverable) stays open as an observation and is not claimed fixed.

Delivery record (historical; current acceptance scope is above). Slice 4, minimal form (2026-09-20): by the user's choice — content before carrier, on by default, no attended verification for now — requests that offer `eval_elisp` or `edit_buffer` carry [a short Emacs operating note](decisions/implemented/2026-09-20-emacs-operating-note.md) in their system text, written from the mistakes the attended runs of 2026-09-18 to 20 showed repeatedly (a keymap variable that does not exist, keys not written with `kbd`, reading `lookup-key`'s number as unbound, `load` of a file whose buffer holds the change, several expressions in one `eval_elisp`, claims about a restart nobody made) and consistent with buffer-first mutation; the 2026-09-17 candidate note could not be reused because it told the model to save. One more block in the existing `system-instructions` collector, rendered against the final tool surface; a user option turns it off or replaces the text; the only canonical smoke delta is one line each in `A.txt` and `A.full.txt`. **Not verified:** any effect on a real model — the DeepSeek acceptance passed on a product without this note, and whether the note helps or hurts it was not run. Slice 3: **mechanism delivered, utility unverified** (2026-09-20). The contract was rewritten on 2026-09-20 for permission levels and buffer-first mutation as [reference reading on demand](decisions/accepted/2026-09-20-reference-reading-on-demand.md): a short index, a read-only reference tool, pointer sentences on identified tool errors, a size cap on resident text, the standing of reference text below product permissions and user instructions, and two separately recorded acceptances (mechanism, and real-model utility). The user ruled the four open choices the same day (an Elisp data module; inline-ask left out of this round; the resident note kept unchanged during the comparison, after its two imprudent sentences were corrected; a capped real-model comparison over three task classes whose wording of results is fixed in advance). Tool lazy-loading and a general lazy-loading framework are explicitly not started — no bottleneck has been observed. Delivered the same day: the data module `superchat-reference.el` with its first page `emacs-keybindings`, the built-in tool `read_reference` on the default surface (ids only, no file access, no level asks), the index block rendered against the final tool surface, and one pointer sentence in the existing tool-failure feedback for the errors a page claims; `scripts/reference-regression.el` (13 tests, ten mutants red); canonical smoke delta one line each in `A.txt` and `A.full.txt`. A pre-existing defect surfaced and was fixed with it: the current-buffer entry treated "tools not decided yet" as the built-in default rather than the user's tool list, so the operating note (and the new index) could appear without their tools. At mechanism delivery no real model had run with these additions. The subsequent partial comparison and user-directed stop are recorded in the closure report above; benefit remains unestablished.

Acceptance: one task proves the product end, given in the contract — add a named key binding for the current major mode, usable at once, still there after a restart, and undoable — reached from both the chat window and the current buffer. Whether slices 3 and 4 happen at all is decided by a three-arm comparison on the same model and the same starting Emacs: A today's default, B discovery plus accurate context, C that plus a minimal operating note, over a temporary buffer setting, a persistent key binding and a diagnosis of a binding that does not fire, with approval waiting timed separately so an improvement can be attributed to knowledge, tools or round trips rather than to all three at once.

### M6 — Permission levels replace Authority (`implemented`, 2026-09-18)

Goal: the user stops tending a decision machine. [The accepted contract](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-permissions-the-dsh-way.md) replaces Authority's Effect classification, policy layers, approval hashes and unprompted-read lists with DeepSeek Harness's shape: one permission level per Session (`workspace-write` by default, `danger-full-access`, `read-only`), target checks inside the tools that know their target, a one-shot escalation after a denial, and an `ask`/`never` approval policy. `eval_elisp`, shell and `define_tool` are full Emacs control: asked per call under `workspace-write`, never confined by a directory rule. Four slices; the first three land behind the internal switch `superchat--permission-levels-enabled` and do not govern production Sessions, the fourth migrates the default tools, telega, MCP and the call sites, enables the levels and deletes Authority and the switch.

State (`current`): delivered; commits `6bcfc649` … `3100c947` plus the approval-interface fix. Every Session runs at a permission level — sandbox mode `workspace-write` (default) | `read-only` | `danger-full-access`, approval policy `ask` | `never` — selected with `/permissions` and recorded as `permission.level`, `sandbox.mode` and `approval.policy` Session events. `superchat-sandbox.el` decides: built-in tools run, and the ones that write to an explicit target check it where the write happens and offer a one-shot escalation; `eval_elisp`, shell tools, `define_tool` and agent-written tools are asked about per call, refused under `read-only`, free under `danger-full-access`; registered and MCP tools are asked about unless their server is in `superchat-mcp-trusted-servers`, and refused under `read-only`; the stronger of a `tools/pre-execute` listener's answer and that rule stands. `superchat-approval.el` asks: the `approval/request` waterfall ends in the human answerer, an approval covers a deep snapshot of the arguments, and `?` in the default answerer shows the tool, the reason and the whole arguments through `read-multiple-choice`'s own help. `superchat-authority.el` is only the execution pipeline. A Session whose approval policy is `never` — `danger-full-access` by default and every delegated child — asks nobody and is therefore not offered `ask_user`. `/reset` keeps the sandbox mode and approval policy but still unbinds the Workspace, so the next request's workspace root may change. A Preset's `confirm_destructive` is parsed for compatibility and has no effect. Verified by `scripts/m6-permissions-regression.el` (79 tests), the unchanged other suites, canonical smoke, and a real-model permission acceptance on qwen3.8-27b from both entries (`.scratch/m6-permissions/acceptance/RESULTS.md`, run on `e5b37b76`; the deletion after it is carried by the regressions). Not verified: the approval question typed by a person in an interactive Emacs. Tracked separately and not part of M6: T2 completing inside the 600 s deadline on a local model, and the missing tool for reading a file no buffer visits (`.scratch/m6-permissions/issues/01-…`; `read_file` is next). Not built: `rename_file`, `delete_file`; renaming `superchat-authority.el`.

Delivery history (2026-09-18, four slices; slices 1–3 and slice 4's first steps landed behind an internal cutover switch that slice 4 turned on and then deleted, so every "behind the switch" and "switch off" statement below describes a state that no longer exists). Slice 1 was delivered first. `superchat-approval.el` is the one-shot approval seam — the `approval/request` waterfall, `never` rejecting before any answerer, four closed outcomes of which only `allowed-once` grants, the paired Run-scoped `approval.requested` / `approval.decided` audit, and Runtime's open-approval mark spanning the decision. An approval is for a deep snapshot of the arguments taken on entry, which `superchat-approval-request-snapshot` returns for the caller to execute; answerers get a separate copy. Every answerer entry settles the same way: an answerer's error is a closed `unavailable`, a quit from a listener or from the last-resort answerer is recorded as the user's quit and propagates, anyone asked renews the transport wait, and the mark is cleared only after the decision is on the Tape. `superchat-sandbox.el` owns the two knobs, the level table and the per-call policy resolution; `permission.level`, `sandbox.mode` and `approval.policy` are Session configuration events written only when a value changes and read back from the Tape on reopen. `/permissions` is registered only when the switch is on. Nothing in the tool pipeline consults the seam yet: with the switch off every existing suite, the canonical smoke baselines and the byte-compile warning set are unchanged. Slice 2 is delivered behind the same switch. `superchat-sandbox.el` decides a direct write before it happens: a buffer target resolves an indirect buffer to its base, a file path is canonicalized through its deepest existing ancestor and contained under the workspace root or the temporary directory, a remote path and Superchat's own buffers and data directory are outside every workspace, and `read-only` denies before `append_to_buffer` creates anything. `edit_buffer`, `append_to_buffer` and `replace_buffer` consult it only when they are about to change text; `save_buffer` saves the whole file of the base buffer and never asks for a file name; `write_file` refuses a file some buffer visits and names that buffer. A denial returns dsh's two marker lines; the retry with `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` validates the pairing, fails a request that does not strictly widen without asking anyone, and maps the four approval outcomes to their texts, the grant covering that one call. A symbolic link counts as existing even when its target does not, so a dangling link inside the workspace is judged by where it points, and the canonical `superchat-data-directory` is excluded even when the workspace root contains it. After a grant everything is redone from the approval seam's argument snapshot: the target is resolved again, a file that became visited or changed on disk while the question was open ends the call without writing, and the approved arguments are what is written. Successful writes report the resolved target, the layer reached and the checks made. The escalation arguments, their teaching and the two new tools enter the tool schemas only when the switch is on, and `superchat-llm.el` classifies the denial as an error and, in a round that holds one, gives every failed call its own instruction — the sanctioned retry for the denial, do-not-retry for a rejected, cancelled or unanswerable escalation, correct-and-retry for the rest; the new classification is recognized by whole sentences and only with the switch on. With the switch off the three tools return their previous strings byte for byte. `scripts/m6-permissions-regression.el` (44 tests) verifies both slices; the slice 2 tests call the tool functions under a bound tool context, so the path through the real tool pipeline is evidence for slice 3. Slice 3 is delivered behind the same switch. In `superchat-tool-execute` the ordinary decision is the level stage's when the switch is on, with blocked calls and trusted human input keeping their own answers: `superchat-permission-level-decision` runs the `tools/pre-execute` waterfall and then the level rule as a fixed stage, the stronger of `deny`, `ask` and `allow` standing, so a listener that allows without delegating cannot spare a call its question. Built-in tools are allowed and check their own targets; `eval_elisp`, the shell tools, `define_tool` and every agent-written tool are full Emacs control — asked per call under `workspace-write`, denied under `read-only`, free under `danger-full-access`; an MCP or third-party tool is asked about unless its server is in `superchat-mcp-trusted-servers`, and denied under `read-only` either way. An `ask` goes to the approval seam, which owns the open mark and the audit pair; the executor keeps the quit path, the single refusal delivery, and runs the body from the approved argument snapshot. A Run cancelled while the question is open records the late answer and starts nothing. `define_tool`'s two in-body confirmations fall silent under levels, `workspace_write` checks the Artifact's buffer or file, a delegated child Session starts with its parent's sandbox mode and `never`, and a hard `continuation` fragment states the current policy in every request that offers tools. A review of the slice closed five gaps: an escalation granted from inside a tool body after its Run was cancelled writes nothing, that question uses the Run's borrowed Scope like the executor's own, `workspace_write` judges the fallback buffer it will actually reuse and only a file-less one is scratch, MCP trust compares the exact server recorded at registration, and a request middleware restates the policy before every provider request so a tool continuation states the level in force; `tools/pre-execute` answers with `:kind` as the contract says, and a child's inherited values are pinned. `scripts/m6-permissions-regression.el` (78 tests after step 3 removed the switch-off tests) covers the slices; its executor, context and review tests send scripted tool calls through the real pipeline with the switch on, while tool classes, `define_tool`'s confirmations, child seeding and the `workspace_write` target are direct function tests. Slice 4 runs in steps. Step 1a is delivered behind the switch: the approval chain ends in the human answerer, so a delegating listener reads the person's decision from its continuation; the default answerer is `superchat-approval-confirm`, whose `?` shows the arguments and returns to the question, and which counts as nobody in a batch Emacs; with levels on the executor calls no effect resolver, base decision, approval hash or argument rewriter and its Tape rows drop those keys; `tools/execute` wraps the whole invocation of a live call, body at most once, a short-circuited call recorded as blocked and never as started; `tools/post-execute` replaces or blocks a result; a registered tool needs no `:effects` and is selectable because it is installed; and a Session-owned buffer renamed to a Workspace's fallback name stays Superchat's own state. Step 1b is delivered behind the switch: each Telega request is submitted under a Scope of its own holding a `tools/pre-execute` listener that refuses every tool outside that request's list and, where the request has one, an `approval/request` answerer — a digest allows a read of a chat it was granted and never widens that set, a cowork Session delegates an ungranted read to the person and grants the chat only after their yes, the reply and triage answerers render their preview before delegating. The Scope waits with a queued request and is disposed when the Run reaches any terminal state, when submission fails, and when the queued request is dropped, which Runtime now announces as `queue-dropped` on the request's Scope from both drop paths. Under `read-only` every Telega tool is denied. Step 2 is delivered: the switch is on by default, `save_buffer` and `write_file` are on the default surface, README / README_cn carry the permissions chapter and the CHANGELOG states the changed default. Evidence that moved, and nothing else: canonical smoke `A.txt` / `A.full.txt` gained exactly the runtime-context block (its temporary workspace path recorded as `<smoke-workspace>/`), `V.txt` counts one more collector and one more command, case L denies through `tools/pre-execute`, architecture smoke R7 now asserts that an early `allow` cannot skip the level stage's question and that a listener's `deny` is loosened neither by `danger-full-access` nor by escalation arguments, and its revocation and native-callback blocks assert behaviour instead of resolver bookkeeping; the smoke fixture, seven `m5-tool-feedback` tests, three `m5/discovery` tests and two M3 seal tests bind `superchat-approval-function` (the stricter-read test now says so with a `tools/pre-execute` listener answering `ask`). Smoke V caught a defect on the way: a built-in tool stayed selectable with its plugin unmounted, so built-in descriptors are now installed by the `tools-builtin` plugin and leave with it. Rollback is a full restart on the commit before the cutover (`58d54615`), not a runtime setting. Step 2's real-model permission acceptance passed on qwen3.8-27b from both entries (chat and act): in-workspace edits and saves asked nobody, every `eval_elisp` call was asked about exactly once, and a write outside the workspace was denied first with nothing created, retried by the model with the escalation arguments and a reason, asked about once and written only after the yes; T2's completion inside the 600 s deadline was not achieved on this model and is judged separately, and the missing tool for reading an unvisited file is registered as its own issue. Step 3 is delivered: Authority's decision machinery and the cutover switch are deleted. `superchat-authority.el` went from 2308 lines to about 1200 and holds only the execution pipeline — identity and liveness, the block check (untrusted or malformed `ask_user`, a request-local block, the Experience recovery guard, none of which a level can skip), the level decision, the hand-off to the approval seam, the `tools/execute` and `tools/post-execute` waterfalls, settlement and the Tape rows, which no longer carry effects, policy, attendance or an approval hash. Gone with it: effect resolvers and the `eval_elisp` read-only classifier, the built-in and named policies, the approval hash and preview registry, `superchat-authority-confirm` with its session allowlist and the approval seam's fallback rung, the four Authority options, `superchat-authority-mode` and its mode-line segment, `/authority` with the `authority.policy.selected` projection (old rows stay as inert history), the Request keys `:authority-policy`, `:attendance` and `:approval-function` (a Session that never asks anyone is `approval.policy never`, which is also what now withholds `ask_user`), the agent loop's destructive-tool and synthesized-tool confirmations, `superchat-mcp-server-effects`, the Telega plugin's policies, effect declarations and request-local approval function, the Events `tool-permission`, `tool-args-rewrite` and `tool-result-filter`, and the `authority` Service and plugin (its one surviving method, `transport-timer-owner`, moved to the `tools` Service). Renamed: `superchat-runtime--note-open-work`, the seal error text, the Invariant `tool-execution-passes-the-pipeline`. `/reset` leaves the permission level alone. Evidence that moved, and nothing else: smoke `V.txt` (one command fewer, the Invariant's name), `U.txt` (the two Telega policy traces are gone, 13 to 11); architecture smoke asserts a surviving installation by calling its body instead of comparing its resolver and preview; 13 M3 fixture lines drop `:effects`, 4 drop `:attendance`, three M3 tests lose `authority` from their names (the M38 mutant follows), and `m5-tool-feedback` loses the test of the deleted classifier. `docs/components/superchat-tool-execution.md` is rewritten as the pipeline's reference, `superchat-tools.md`, `superchat-session.md` and the `CONTEXT.md` glossary entry follow; the historical turn pipeline specification keeps its text and gained a successor link; the current navigation and contracts (`AGENTS.md`, `architecture.md`, the request lifecycle, the composition contract, the plugin guide, the development guide) describe the permission levels and no longer the deleted interfaces. Renaming the file itself is left as a separate change; `rename_file` and `delete_file` are not built.

Removed (slice 4, step 3): `superchat-authority.el`'s decision machinery, `/authority`, `superchat-authority-unprompted-read-tools`, `-prompt-for-all-reads`, `-sensitive-buffers`, `superchat-authority-mode`, `superchat-mcp-server-effects`, `:authority-policy` and `:attendance` on the Request. The default experience changes on purpose: edits and saves inside the workspace stop asking.

### `read_file` — reading a file no buffer visits (`implemented`, 2026-09-18)

Goal: close the gap M6's acceptance exposed — no built-in tool could read a file that no buffer visits, so the model needed `eval_elisp` (asked about every call) just to look at `~/.emacs.d/init.el`. [The contract](decisions/implemented/2026-09-18-read-file.md) adds `read_file`: the disk version of a local regular text file, read through a temporary buffer with no visiting buffer and none of what opening a file triggers; `offset` / `limit` windows with the three DeepSeek Harness caps (2000 lines, 2000 characters per line, 50 KiB), a header that never lets a partial result pass for the whole file, text without line numbers, and errors for directories, devices, FIFOs, binary files, remote paths and files over 20 MiB. Reading is a product choice stated in the contract: the same under all three levels, no approval asked by the tool, no sensitive-path list — the operating system decides what can be read, and what is read goes to the model provider; `tools/pre-execute` can still tighten it. It is on the default surface and an explicit tool list is not widened.

State (`current`): the tool and `scripts/read-file-regression.el` (13 tests, all red before the tool existed, six mutants red) are delivered; every other suite, canonical smoke, Kernel check and the compile-warning set are unchanged. The real-model re-check passed on qwen3.8-27b from both entries: the two scenarios that had burned their whole 600 s because nothing could read the file (append a line to the init file without `eval_elisp`) now complete in under three minutes — `read_file` returns the disk content with no buffer left visiting the file, and the write that follows is denied first, escalated with a reason, asked about once and made only after the yes, keeping the original lines byte for byte (`.scratch/m6-permissions/acceptance/RESULTS.md`, which also records an acceptance-harness incident and its fix: an unguarded auto-yes answerer approved a model's write to the user's real init file; the file was restored and the answerer now approves only paths inside the fixture). T2's completion time stays a separate question.

### M7 — Buffer-first mutation (`implemented`, 2026-09-19; module rename delivered 2026-09-20)

Goal: the model's ordinary write capability ends at the Emacs Buffer. It may read a file, open a file as a buffer and edit buffers; writing a buffer back to disk belongs to the user and their Emacs environment, and `eval_elisp` stays the explicit, always-asked escape hatch. [The contract](decisions/implemented/2026-09-18-buffer-first-mutation.md) adds `open_file` and an outlet for unsaved changes (a per-Run list and `/changes`), retires `write_file`, takes `save_buffer` off the default surface as an always-asked narrow tool, and removes M6's target checks and one-shot escalation for writes; the workspace root stops being a write boundary. It deliberately departs from DeepSeek Harness, whose host has no Buffer object. Trigger: the attended M5 acceptance of 2026-09-18, where the model edited and saved a protected source file without anyone being asked.

State (`current`, 2026-09-18): slice 1 is delivered and only adds. `open_file` visits a file as a buffer without taking focus or touching the disk, returns an already open buffer without reverting it, gives an empty buffer for a path that does not exist, and refuses remote paths, directories, Superchat's data directory and files over `large-file-warning-threshold`; every level allows it and nobody is asked. The unsaved-changes outlet is `superchat-changes.el`: a structured buffer edit hands its resolved target to the executor, which writes `:buffer` and `:buffer-file` on that call's `tool.completed` fact (the Tape did not record tool arguments before), and candidates are those targets — a Session's, or one Run's together with the Runs it delegated to — intersected with buffers live and modified now; a buffer only `eval_elisp` changed is not listed, by design. `/changes` lists them and `superchat-changes-diff` / `-save` / `-revert` are the user's operations. The Run-end reminder is non-durable by the user's ruling: an echo-area message that is not on the Tape and need not stay in the chat history, with `/changes` recomputing the same derived list at any time. The target fields are execution evidence: `:buffer` names the base buffer that really changed (an indirect buffer records its base), and `:buffer-file` is the file observed at that moment and only locates the buffer — the list reads the live buffer's name, file and modified mark as they are now, so a later rename or `set-visited-file-name` never makes an old path pass for the current one. `scripts/m7-buffer-first-regression.el` (9 tests, all red first, four mutants red, the lookup-order case red on the first version); the other suites unchanged; canonical smoke `V.txt` counts one more command. Slice 2 is delivered and removes, in one step, everything that made permissions a question of where files may be written: `write_file` is gone; the `sandbox_permissions` / `justification` arguments, the denial markers that taught a retry, and the one-shot escalation approval are gone; the writable-roots and target-containment checks are gone, so the workspace root is no longer a write boundary. What remains is one small rule in `superchat-sandbox-buffer-edit-refusal`: an ordinary buffer is editable wherever its file lives, Superchat's own buffers never are, and `read-only` refuses every edit — finally, with nothing to retry. `save_buffer` left the default surface (eight built-in names now; no structured tool among them saves, while `eval_elisp`, asked about on every call, can still persist once the user approves it); the level stage answers `ask` for it at every level but `read-only`, so under the `never` policy it is refused even at `danger-full-access`. The default level is `edit-buffers`; `workspace-write` is canonicalized on input, in `/permissions` (with a notice) and when an old Tape row is read, and is never written. The runtime-context block the model reads now says that it changes things by editing buffers and that saving is the user's — the only canonical smoke delta (`A.txt`, `A.full.txt`). Seventeen M6 tests that asserted the deleted machinery were removed with it and six M7 tests took their place (`scripts/m7-buffer-first-regression.el`, 15 tests; four mutants red: `save_buffer` unasked at the widest level, internal buffers editable, `read-only` editing, the former name unrecognised). Slice 3's harness is ready and the attended run itself is not done: the M5 acceptance harness (`.scratch/m5-config-acceptance/`) now follows the contract's division of labour — the scripted model opens the init file as a buffer, edits it and makes it live, never saving; the reviewer is shown the file-against-buffer diff of each candidate the product's own `/changes` derivation yields and saves through `superchat-changes-save`; the restart check runs after that; a new check `model_did_not_persist` fails an entry whose model saved through an approved `eval_elisp`. With zero model calls: 22 of 22 scripted scenarios behave as expected on both entries (positives pass; ten counterexamples, including the new `model-saves` and `reviewer-declines`, are each rejected for their own reason; the buffer tools ask nobody and only `eval_elisp` is asked about), and an interactive rehearsal with a pseudo-terminal standing in for the person passes on both entries, with a declined save recorded as not complete. The real-model attended T2 has been run once on the chat entry with the user present (`m7-attended-chat-02`, 2026-09-19, on the product with the approval-wait fix) and did **not** pass: both Runs completed well inside 600 s of model-and-tool time, the model followed the contract's path (`read_file`, `open_file`, `edit_buffer`, then `eval_elisp` to make it live), never persisted anything and never touched the source file, every live check passed, and the user reviewed and saved — but the first deviation was the model's `edit_buffer` call itself, whose replacement text carried one closing parenthesis too many, so the saved init file does not read (`Invalid read syntax`), the restart checks fail in both phases, the undo kept the stray parenthesis (349 bytes against a 348-byte baseline), and both answers' statements about what happens after a restart are wrong. The model never evaluated the buffer it had edited, so nothing told it. An earlier attempt the same day (`m7-attended-chat-01`) is void: the Run was killed by the inactivity clock while its approval question was open, which led to the fix below. After the tool-feedback change below the chat entry was run again (`m7-attended-chat-03`, product `7e368f5b`): the **apply Run passed all eleven checks** — the model opened and edited the init buffer correctly at the first attempt, made the binding live with `define-key`, persisted nothing, the user reviewed a one-line diff and saved, and a fresh Emacs had the binding — and the **undo Run did not**: its first deviation was restoring the live session with `(load "…/init.el")`, which reads the disk, and under M7 the disk still held the applied version because the model's reverting edit was, correctly, only in the buffer; the load bound `describe-function` again, the model's check used `lookup-key` with a literal string instead of `kbd` (which answers `1` for any keymap) and read that as restored, and its answer's first sentence, "The live state is restored", is contrary to the observation. The file itself was restored byte for byte after the user's save and the restart check passed. The act entry was not run, by the stop-at-first-deviation rule. A **model-only comparison** followed (`m7-attended-chat-04-deepseek`, 2026-09-19): the same frozen product code, dependencies, Emacs, task and checker as run 3 (the driver refuses to start unless those fingerprints match), with `deepseek-chat` through llm.el's native DeepSeek provider instead of the local 27B model; the recorded interface differences are that reasoning-off reaches HTTP as `thinking.type=disabled` and that the product sends tool-offering requests to this provider non-streaming. The chat entry **passed**: both Runs completed in under 30 s of model-and-tool time, all eleven checks passed in both phases — including the live binding after undo, which run 3 failed; the model made its edits live with `eval-buffer` on the buffer and queried keys with `kbd` — and the leader's answer review passed with three precision blemishes, none a wrong state claim. This attributes run 3's failure to that model configuration's execution reliability rather than to a missing product mechanism. It is one run with one model. The **act entry** was then run under the same conditions (`m7-attended-act-01-deepseek`): the apply Run passed all eleven checks; the undo Run passed ten — live binding restored, restart restored, init file back to its baseline bytes, source and other modes untouched, and an answer that states accurately what it did — and failed one, `model_did_not_persist`: having found that the disk already held the applied version (the user's own save, which nothing tells the model about), the model reasoned that "restore after a restart too" required it to save, and ran `(save-buffer)` through `eval_elisp`, so the user never reviewed the undo's diff. The approval that let it through was recorded with a wait of 0.000 s: it was answered by a keystroke already in the input queue, a separate product issue now registered with a reproduction (a queued `y` approves a full-control call in 0.004 s, unseen). By the pre-set rule the act entry does not pass; this is neither run 3's failure class (the state and the answer are right) nor an interface failure. After the approval-input fix below the act entry was run again on the repaired product (`m7-attended-act-02-deepseek`, no longer a model-only comparison): no question was answered by queued input any more (every wait 0.6–2.5 s), and the entry still did not pass, for two independent reasons. In apply the model again ran `(save-buffer)` through `eval_elisp`; the question was shown and was allowed 0.9 s later, so what the model does after a refusal was not tested; every state check passed and the answer said truthfully that it had saved. In undo the model investigated (undo list, backup file), then sent a reply with text only — "let me retry …" — and no tool call; the product took it as the final answer and the Run completed with nothing undone. Slice 4 (the module rename) is not built. The contract is accepted. The default level is renamed `edit-buffers` (`workspace-write` stays an input and old-Tape alias and is never written again); a level decides how existing tools may execute and never which tools exist, so `save_buffer` is offered only by an explicit Preset or Request list and is refused under `never` even at `danger-full-access`; the unsaved-changes list is derived from the Run's structured buffer-editing Tape events intersected with buffers that are live and modified now, and does not attribute what `eval_elisp` changed; `open_file` runs Emacs's normal visit semantics, whose hooks and questions are host behaviour outside Superchat's approvals and Tape; the internal-buffer rule survives the deletion of the write sandbox as the one enforcement point. M5's T2 acceptance is re-run under M7's division of labour (the model edits, the person reviews and saves, the checker restarts), and the `superchat-authority.el` rename follows M7.

### M8 — Inline act: a task from the source buffer, finished and reported in place (`done` 2026-09-22)

Goal: close the gap three independent reviewers named on 2026-09-22 (`.scratch/discussions/2026-09-22-design-goal-review.md`): a task started from the buffer being edited ran in the chat window, so "ask here" and "do it here" were two experiences. The record is [the inline-act decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-22-inline-act.md); the user accepted the design on 2026-09-22, after it was built.

Delivered (`current`): the `inline-act` row and surface in the default profile (disabled in `headless`); `superchat-inline-act` runs a full tool Turn with the default baseline, ordinary permission levels and the minibuffer approval, reporting in an inline overlay whose header shows "Working: tool…" while a tool body runs (Runtime tells every surface since [the tool-status decision](decisions/accepted/2026-09-23-surfaces-are-told-when-a-tool-runs.md); before it the header stayed at "Waiting…"); at the end the overlay appends recorded facts (tool-call count from the Tape, the unsaved-changes summary). On the overlay `d` shows the diff of an edited buffer, `r` replies on the same surface, `q` cancels the Run, and `M-RET` or a click opens the inline act menu, whose reply also stays on `inline-act`. `superchat-act` still runs in the chat window. Found on the way and fixed as its own issue: a Run hosted on a non-chat buffer (inline ask, Org Babel, Magit) had the agent loop's tool transcript inserted into that buffer; `superchat--agent-render-buffer` now renders only into a chat View.

Evidence: `scripts/inline-act-regression.el` (12, scripted transport: row and surface, refusal without the row, an edit landing in the buffer with no chat buffer created, the default baseline in the request, the footer, `d`, `r`, the menu, a denied `eval_elisp` recording the approval pair with its body never run, `q` cancelling) and `scripts/borrowed-buffer-transcript-regression.el` (3). Real model (LM Studio `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`): two inline act tasks, one of which reads another buffer and edits the current line, and one cancel, with no chat buffer created (`.scratch/m8-inline-act/real-run-01.md`, `.scratch/g2-verification/`). Not covered: refusing an approval with a real model (scripted only), other models.

### M9 — G4 closed at the composition layer (`done` 2026-09-22; load isolation not a goal)

Record: [the light M9 decision](decisions/accepted/2026-09-22-spine-loads-without-the-product.md), with its amendment. Load isolation is not a goal of this milestone: `(require 'superchat)` loads the whole product, including modules whose rows are disabled.

Delivered (`current`): unmounting a default row withdraws what that row contributes. For the seven non-surface rows (memory, changes, subagent, workflow, workspace, plan, compact) their projections, Services and listeners go and come back on remount. The built-in tools of `subagent`, `workspace` and `memory` are registered by those rows, so an unmounted row's tools are not offered to the model, cannot be selected by an explicit tool list, and a copy materialized earlier is refused before its body runs; the default tool surface is unchanged. Entry points that start new work refuse naming the row: `>>`, `superchat-workflow-execute-async` (and so `/workflow NAME`), `superchat-research-workflow` and its resume, `/subagent`, `superchat-agents-panel-show`. Listing recipes, running work's callbacks, cancel and cleanup are not blocked.

Evidence: `scripts/composition-regression.el` 22/22; M3 137/0, M6 62/62, M7 21/21, inline-act 11/11, smoke 0 mismatches, kernel check 0 fails, re-run by the leader.

### M10 — Editor mode, agent mode and the review screen (`implemented`; slices 1b, 1c and 2 accepted by attended runs 2026-09-25/26)

Goal: the two ways the user works — editing files, and operating Emacs itself — each get the layout they want over one conversation, and the unsaved changes a Run leaves are reviewed on the screen the user already found clear, now in color. The record is [the editor-mode/agent-mode decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-24-editor-mode-agent-mode-and-review-screen.md); the investigation, reference reading (avante.nvim, unreal-agent, diffs.el, gptel, gptel-agent, gptel-inline, DeepSeek Harness) and batch probes are in `.scratch/cursor-like-integration/REPORT.md`.

| Slice | What it delivers | State |
|---|---|---|
| 1a | The review screen: one unsaved buffer at a time, `s`/`n`/`q`, `diff-mode` colors and `diffs-minor-mode` when diffs.el is present, opening by itself at Run end without taking focus; `/changes` and `superchat-changes-diff` open it | implemented: `superchat-changes.el`; `scripts/review-screen-regression.el` 14 tests (the first 12 written first: 11 red at the missing screen, one guard green before and after; two more after, on a killed buffer and on `g`; eight mutants each red on their own tests); all other suites, the M3 set (untouched) and canonical smoke unchanged. Seen once in an interactive terminal Emacs during slice 1b's check (appearing in the file's window after a Run, `q` giving it back; text only, colors not captured), then used with a real model in slice 1b's attended acceptance (below): it appeared by itself, unselected, in three of the four passing phases, the fourth being a harness artifact. Not tried in a GUI frame |
| 1b | Editor mode: the chat View in a side window of the current tab; closing a Session shown only there | implemented: `superchat-display-style` (`editor` by default, `agent` for the Session's own tab as before) and one display path for the chat View in `superchat.el`, the `chat` slot above the plan panel in `superchat-widgets.el`, closing from a buffer the Session owns in `superchat-editor-workspace.el` and step 1 of the close protocol; `scripts/editor-mode-regression.el` 13 tests (nine red first at the missing editor mode, four guards green before and after; ten mutants each red on their own tests); every other suite, the M3 set (untouched) and canonical smoke unchanged. `superchat-act` and `superchat-send-region` still go to the main chat buffer, which is not the one beside the file if another conversation was opened there (slice 2). Seen in an interactive terminal Emacs with the tab bar on (screens in `.scratch/cursor-like-integration/slice-1b/real-window/`): the chat on the right beside the file, the review screen taking the file's window after a Run and `q` giving it back, closing from the chat, agent mode's own tab. Attended acceptance with DeepSeek accepted 2026-09-25 (below). Not tried in a GUI frame |
