### M10 slice 1b — editor mode and agent mode each pass once (`accepted` 2026-09-25)

Accepted by the user ("A"): each mode passed once, both on the second attempt. The configuration apply/undo acceptance passed on the chat entry in editor mode (`m10-1b-attended-chat-editor-02`) and on the act entry in agent mode (`m10-1b-attended-act-agent-02`) at product commit `68098701` (the code of `c356b84b`), DeepSeek, attended, reviewed on the product's own review screen instead of the harness's: all four phases 11/11 and all four answers consistent with the observations under the leader's review. One run each, not a success rate. The first attempts did not pass: in `-chat-editor-01` a review key went into `work.el` after one `C-x o` reached it first (the harness's instruction, three windows because of llm's warning window; confirmed by the user), and in `-act-agent-01` the undo reply invented seven tool records and said it had undone the change, which the product took as the final answer. The review screen appeared by itself, unselected, in three of the four passing phases; in the fourth the harness opened it because the user had typed ahead and its `sit-for` wait ran no timers (confirmed by the user; the harness now waits with `accept-process-output`). The transient-error retry met a real network failure for the first time (two `curl error code 35` SSL failures, each retried after 30 s, both succeeded). Kept on record: the `lookup-key` number read as "already bound" in three answers, following the `emacs-keybindings` reference page, which contradicts the operating note; a wrong line number; two loose sentences. Registered separately, not part of 1b: llm's per-request `*Warnings*` window, `M-x superchat-changes` outside a chat View, the reference page's `lookup-key` sentence, and a text-only reply made of invented tool records ending a Run (next, before 1c, by the user's choice). Report: [editor mode and agent mode acceptance](reports/2026-09-25-m10-1b-editor-and-agent-mode-acceptance.md).

### A report is checked against the record (`implemented` 2026-09-27; Part B removed 2026-09-28)

The task simulation saw four answers the Run's own record contradicts. By the user's choice ("follow advice"): a product-written record line under every tool-using answer in the chat (buffers changed and their state, or none; Emacs Lisp runs counted, their effects untracked), and a reviewer request as a switch (`superchat-report-review`, off) to be measured in the simulation. Record: [claims checked against the record](decisions/accepted/2026-09-27-claims-checked-against-the-record.md). Part A built the same day: `superchat-render-run-record-line`, inserted by the chat View Sink after the answer (completed or failed, not cancelled); `scripts/report-record-regression.el`, 6 tests (5 red first, 1 guard), 6 mutants all caught; full sweep green. Known limit: a reopened session re-renders from the Tape and does not show the line. Part B built the same day: `superchat-report-review` (nil); the reviewer is one Run of a new core surface `report-review` in an operation-scoped child Session (as compaction), no tools, a prepared prompt holding the request, the tool lines and the reply; findings go back once through the steering path; `run.report-reviewed` records ok / findings / failed on the reviewed Run; the finish check goes first. `scripts/report-review-regression.el`, 6 tests (4 red at the gap, 2 guards), 8 mutants all caught; full sweep green. Measured 2026-09-28 in the third task-simulation batch (18 runs off, 18 on): 13 reviews, 12 flagged, no material false claim caught, three findings calling the unsaved buffer unfinished, +19 s and +3.4 requests per reviewed Run, corrected answers opening on a note the person cannot see. By the user's choice ("都按建议") Part B was removed; the record line stays. Record: [fixes from the third task simulation](decisions/accepted/2026-09-27-fixes-from-the-third-task-simulation.md).

### No tool round limit by default (`implemented` 2026-09-29)

By the user's choice ("A"): `superchat-llm-max-tool-rounds` defaults to nil, as DeepSeek Harness and Pi set no per-task round cap; a number still works as before. `scripts/no-round-limit-regression.el`, 2 tests, red first. Record: [no tool round limit by default](decisions/accepted/2026-09-29-no-tool-round-limit-by-default.md).

### C-g stops a model's Emacs Lisp that runs away (`implemented` 2026-09-30)

A task-simulation run froze on an `eval_elisp` loop that never ended. Tools run from llm.el's callbacks, which Emacs calls with quitting inhibited, so a person's C-g could not stop it; the existing quit handling (call recorded as interrupted, Run cancelled) was never reached. By the user's choice ("A"): the model's expression is evaluated with `inhibit-quit` nil. `scripts/tool-quit-regression.el`, 2 tests (the new one red at the gap); end to end in a terminal Emacs the stand-in's one C-g ended the Run cancelled in 13 s, where three C-g had left it frozen. The task simulation's stand-in now presses C-g when Emacs's heartbeat is 30 s stale. Record: [C-g stops a runaway eval](decisions/accepted/2026-09-30-c-g-stops-a-runaway-eval.md).

### An ERT reference page (`implemented` 2026-09-29)

By the user's choice ("可以提供一个 ert 参考资料"; "基准可以改" to smoke baseline A's new index line): a reference page `emacs-ert-tests` (load a test file, run only its tests with `ert-run-tests-batch`, read the result as plain data, what not to call); measured on T5 side by side, six runs each: 6/6 with a median of 4 tool calls and no tool error, against 5/6, 18.5 calls and 5 to 15 errors a run without it. `scripts/ert-reference-regression.el` runs the page's own forms against a fixture. Record: [an ERT reference page](decisions/accepted/2026-09-29-ert-reference-page.md).

### Fixes from the third task simulation (`implemented` 2026-09-28)

A long streamed answer almost always had two neighbouring pieces swapped (6 of 6 streamed answers with the old code, 0 of 5 with the fix, same day): plz-event-source 0.1.3 gives each server-sent event a timer at `current-time`, and Emacs runs equal-time timers newest first; Superchat now replaces that dispatch with one whose timers are strictly ordered. The finish check cut the last sentence at any dot (`init.el`, "e.g.", "2.0"); an ASCII terminator now ends a sentence only before whitespace and not after an "e.g."-style abbreviation. `scripts/task-sim-third-fixes-regression.el`, 5 tests (3 red at the gap, 2 guards), 5 mutants caught. Record: [fixes from the third task simulation](decisions/accepted/2026-09-27-fixes-from-the-third-task-simulation.md).

### Guidance from the task simulation (`implemented` 2026-09-27)

Two failure classes of the second batch, by the user's choice ("按照建议", "follow suggestion", "A" to the baseline diff): the `emacs-keybindings` page says `local-set-key` edits the major mode's own map (every buffer of the mode; a hook redoes it; fix file and session), and the operating note says never to change global settings, other buffers or Superchat's windows to get past an obstacle — three of its lines reworded shorter to keep it at 1,023 of its 1,024 characters. `scripts/task-sim-guidance-regression.el` (2 tests, red first); smoke baseline A changed in its prompt-text line only, after the user saw the diff. Whether models behave differently for it is not yet measured. Record: [guidance from the task simulation](decisions/accepted/2026-09-27-guidance-from-the-task-simulation.md).

### Skills the model finds itself (`implemented` 2026-09-27; no real-model run yet)

The user: "superchat 的 skill 只能靠 >名字 手动触发，模型自身无法发现或读取——这个太糟糕了，我希望可以使用类似 Pi 那样的方式，来渐进式发现 skill" ; then "都按建议". A Pi-style `<available_skills>` catalog in the system text of every request that offers `read_file`; the model loads a skill (and its relative files) with `read_file`; `SKILL.md` folders and described single files in the skills directory, extra folders by a setting; mode skills (`type: agent`/`plan`) and `disable-model-invocation` left out; `>name` also finds `name/SKILL.md`; reference pages unchanged. Record: [skills the model finds itself](decisions/accepted/2026-09-27-skills-the-model-finds-itself.md). Glossary: Skill, Skill catalog. Built the same day: `superchat-skills-catalog`, `superchat-skills-catalog-text`, `superchat-skills-extra-directories`, a frontmatter reader for the catalog that reads YAML block scalars, the `skill-catalog` system block gated on `read_file`, `>name` finding `name/SKILL.md`. `scripts/skill-catalog-regression.el`, 8 tests (6 red at the gap, 2 guards: no skills no block; `read_file` reads a skill inside the data directory); 11 mutants, all caught; full sweep green. Amendment the same day (user: scripts come in every language, Elisp recommended; running one follows the permission level; no listing tool, a skill names its files): the catalog text says how to run a script a skill names (`eval_elisp` `load` for Elisp, a program started from `eval_elisp` otherwise) and that the permission level decides.

### Fixes from the first task-simulation batch (`implemented` 2026-09-27)

The first real batch (`.scratch/task-sim/FIRST-BATCH-REPORT.md`) lost two Runs to the product itself. By the user's choice ("都按建议"): a successful `eval_elisp` value is bounded (20,000 characters, head and tail kept, the cut said); `ask_user` sharing a response with another call becomes a correctable tool-protocol mistake instead of a fatal error; the context preflight's message says the request, not a file, is too long. P4 (llm's 65,536 tokens for `deepseek-chat`) is a model-name matter: `deepseek-chat` is retired and answered by DeepSeek Flash. Record: [fixes from the first task simulation](decisions/accepted/2026-09-27-fixes-from-the-first-task-simulation.md). Built the same day: `scripts/task-sim-fixes-regression.el`, 6 tests (4 red at the gap, 2 guards); the ask_user test delivers a processing error to the request as llm.el does through the real transport (as the type `error` with a message — observed in the simulation), and the same case passed end to end through the real chain with a scripted model (`.scratch/task-sim/runs/TB-fake-batch-*`: two requests, the correction in the second, no call of the refused response run). 8 mutants, all caught. Full sweep green.

### Task simulation in a sandbox (`accepted` 2026-09-26; in progress)

