By the user's choice ("follow", three answers A): `read_buffer` gets `read_file`'s `offset`/`limit` window, its three limits and its header; no line numbers; a narrowed buffer is read in its narrowed part and the header says so; a file buffer with unsaved changes says its text is unsaved. Record: [read_buffer window and caps](decisions/accepted/2026-09-26-read-buffer-window-and-caps.md). Issue `.scratch/issues/2026-09-24-read-buffer-has-no-window-or-cap.md`. Built the same day: one windowing helper serves both reads (`read_file`'s output byte-identical, pinned by a guard). `scripts/read-buffer-regression.el`, 14 tests: 12 red at the gap before the code (among them a scripted Run whose 1.5 MB read made the next request overflow the 128k window, so the Run failed), 2 guards green before and after; 12 mutants, all caught. Tests that pinned the old whole-text result were updated (`m5-tool-feedback`, `session-view`); three fixtures that size compaction with a 400-character budget now use 620 where a stored `read_buffer` line sits in the kept part, so each test keeps the same entries raw as before (checked entry by entry). Full sweep green. Not run with a real model.

